<Page
	title="Now"
	description="What I'm studying, building, and asking right now."
	dateline="Last updated November 2026"
>
	<div class="prose">
		<h2>Studying</h2>
		<p>
			I'm partway through the year at the Eisenhower School at the National Defense University.
			This term that means defense acquisition, the economics of national security, and an
			industry study of finance.
		</p>

		<h2>Researching</h2>
		<p>
			A paper on how allies build defense and dual-use technology together: when a joint
			program office works, when a single lead company works better, and what export controls
			and investment rules do to both.
		</p>

		<h2>Building</h2>
		<p>
			A set of tools built on public data: a Defense Industrial Base Atlas, which is the furthest
			along, and a European Energy Dashboard. Both try to show industrial capacity plainly: who
			can make what, where, and how fast.
		</p>

		<h2>Asking</h2>
		<p>
			Whether the data we already collect can be made to answer new questions. The US has
			world-class economic statistics. But the numbers sit in many places with no way to see
			them together, and a question like who makes a given part, and where, falls between the
			datasets.
		</p>

		<h2>Writing</h2>
		<p>
			I publish here when a piece is ready, and I don't keep a schedule.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
